The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
	kfree(channel);
	list_add_tail(&channel->percpu_list, &hv_cpu->chan_list);
	list_del(&channel->percpu_list);
```
Reason:
The bug occurs because the `free_channel` function frees the `channel` object while it may still be referenced in the per-cpu channel list. If an interrupt occurs during the deletion process, the interrupt routine could access the freed `channel`, leading to a use-after-free condition and undefined behavior.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
	kfree_rcu(channel, rcu);
	list_add_tail_rcu(&channel->percpu_list, &hv_cpu->chan_list);
	list_del_rcu(&channel->percpu_list);
```
Reason:
The fix uses RCU (Read-Copy-Update) protection for managing the per-cpu channel list. By using `kfree_rcu`, the `channel` is safely freed only after all references to it are no longer in use. Similarly, `list_add_tail_rcu` and `list_del_rcu` ensure that modifications to the list are done in a way that prevents access to freed memory, thus eliminating the use-after-free issue.

